ing software. Launching products. Testing pricing strategies. Acquiring customers. Optimizing operations. And doing all of this simultaneously, at a speed no human organization could possibly match. Now let’s take this thought experiment a little further. ⚡ The One-Hour Corporation Imagine a technology giant operating an enormous army of AI agents. • After one second: One million business ideas generated and virtually evaluated. • After one minute: The thousand most promising opportunities identified. • After ten minutes: A hundred products launched, with some already generating their first revenue. • After one hour: Ten functioning businesses operating in real markets. • After ten hours: Several have already been shut down because the agents discovered better opportunities. Of course, this is a hypothetical scenario, not a technological capability we possess today. But the underlying idea is fascinating. What happens to our understanding of entrepreneurship when the lifecycle of a company shrinks from years to hours? What happens to competitive advantage when discovering a profitable opportunity takes milliseconds — and thousands of competing agents discover exactly the same opportunity? And what happens to human entrepreneurs when intelligence itself becomes an industrial resource? 🌍 Welcome to the Communist Paradise? Here’s the paradox. If everyone has access to AI capable of creating successful businesses, shouldn’t everyone have an equal opportunity to become wealthy? Unlimited intelligence. Unlimited entrepreneurial potential. A wonderful technological version of a communist paradise. (A little hello to Elon Musk and his vision of Universal High Income.) Except I don’t believe this paradise will ever arrive. Because access to intelligence and control over intelligence are two very different things. The companies developing the world’s most powerful AI models will sell increasingly capable systems to millions, perhaps billions, of customers. But why would they necessarily give everyone the same capabilities they possess internally? The most advanced models, enormous GPU clusters, specialized infrastructure, and the ability to deploy millions of agents simultaneously could remain concentrated among a relatively small number of companies. Yes, there’s open source. Yes, there’s China. Yes, technological competition will continue. But even if two companies have access to exactly the same AI model, their economic capabilities may differ enormously. One can afford ten agents. Another can afford ten million. And those ten million agents can continuously experiment, compete, learn, and improve their strategies.","image":"","video":"","hasVideo":false}]
